@extends('layouts.app')

@section('title')Views@endsection
@section('description')The panel screens are Propel views in the ignite namespace. An app can add its own views, extend the panel layout, or point the panel at a views folder.@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>The panel's screens are Propel views in the <code>ignite</code> namespace. The app can add its own pages in <code>resources/views/</code>, and they can extend the panel's layout.</p>

  <h2>The ignite namespace</h2>
  <p><code>Ignite::new()</code> registers the <code>ignite</code> namespace. The views in it are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>ignite::layouts.app</code>, the page frame, with the header and navigation.</li>
    <li><code>ignite::login</code>, the sign-in page.</li>
    <li><code>ignite::dashboard</code>, the stat cards and the latest records.</li>
    <li><code>ignite::index</code>, the list of a resource.</li>
    <li><code>ignite::form</code>, the create and edit pages.</li>
    <li><code>ignite::profile</code>, the signed-in user's profile.</li>
    <li><code>ignite::partials.flash</code>, the message shown after a change.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The panel has seven views: the dashboard, the list, the form, the sign-in page, the profile, and the forgot-password and reset-password pages. The layout and the flash partial are shared by them.</p>

  <h2>Extend the layout</h2>
  <p>A view of the app extends the panel's layout and fills its <code>title</code> and <code>content</code> sections. It needs only its own variables. This view shows the signed-in user, and the route in <a href="/docs/panel">Panel</a> renders it with <code>user_id</code>:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@extends('ignite::layouts.app')

@@section('title')
  Account
@@endsection

@@section('content')
  &lt;h1&gt;Account&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Signed in as user @{{ $user_id }}.&lt;/p&gt;
@@endsection</code></pre>
  <p>The view is <code>resources/views/account.propel</code>, and the view name is <code>account</code>.</p>
  <p>The layout also yields three sections for the panel's own chrome. The panel's pages set them, and an app view can set them too:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>ignite_home</code>, the brand link in the header. It defaults to <code>/admin</code>.</li>
    <li><code>ignite_nav</code>, the navigation and the sign-out button. It is empty by default, so an app view has no navigation unless it sets this section.</li>
    <li><code>ignite_flash</code>, the message shown after a change. It is empty by default.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>An app cannot replace one of the panel's views on its own. The only way to change a panel view is to point the panel at a directory, which replaces all of them.</p>

  <h2>Where the views come from</h2>
  <p>A debug build renders the panel's views with Propel's dev renderer, which reads them on each request. A release build renders compiled views only: the panel's build script compiles the five panel views, the dashboard, the index, the form, the login and the profile, into Rust. Both builds embed the views in the <code>ignite-panel</code> binary, so the default needs no view files.</p>
  <p>In a debug build, <code>Ignite::views_dir</code> does this in the builder, and the <code>IGNITE_VIEWS_DIR</code> environment variable does the same from the environment. The builder call wins over the variable. The directory makes the dev renderer read the views from disk, so a developer can edit them without rebuilding. It replaces all of the panel's views, and it must contain <code>layouts/app.propel</code>. <code>fairing()</code> panics when that file is missing. A release build ignores both.</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use ignite_panel::Ignite;

/// The panel with its views read from a directory on disk, so the views can be
/// edited without rebuilding. Without this call, the views are embedded.
pub fn panel() -&gt; Ignite {
    Ignite::new().views_dir("/srv/ignite/views")
}</code></pre>
  <p>An app that renders its own views in release compiles them with its own <code>build.rs</code>, as the panel does, and its page contexts are plain structs. The app's views can extend the panel's layout, <code>ignite::layouts.app</code>. The panel publishes its views directory to the apps that depend on it, through the <code>links</code> key in its manifest, so the app's build script finds it in <code>DEP_IGNITE_PANEL_VIEWS_DIR</code> and compiles the panel's layout with the app's views. The app needs <code>propel_compiler</code> as a build dependency at the same Propel pin. The contract describes this rule in its Views section, and the example app's build script does this:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">//! Compiles the app's views into Rust, so a release build renders them without a
//! template interpreter. The panel's own views are compiled too, because the
//! app's views extend its layout (`ignite::layouts.app`).

fn main() {
    // ignite-panel publishes its views directory to its dependents.
    let panel_views = std::env::var("DEP_IGNITE_PANEL_VIEWS_DIR")
        .expect("ignite-panel publishes its views directory");

    propel_compiler::build::Views::new("resources/views")
        .namespace("ignite", panel_views)
        .asset_namespace("ui")
        .view("home", "crate::Home")
        .view("subscribe", "crate::subscribe::Subscribe")
        .build();
}</code></pre>
  <p>The panel registers its own stylesheet and script, the <code>ui</code> asset namespace, so an app needs no asset step: not in a page helper, not in a view's context. A view that extends the layout is compiled into the app's build, and the layout names <code>ui::</code>, so the app's build script declares the namespace with <code>.asset_namespace("ui")</code>, as the example's build script does. The build fails without that line.</p>
@endsection
